{"study_accession":"PRJNA398556","secondary_study_accession":"SRP115579","title":"Maternal pregravid obesity remodels the DNA methylation landscape of cord blood monocytes disrupting its inflammatory program","description":"Pre-pregnancy maternal obesity is associated with adverse outcomes for the offspring, including increased incidence of neonatal bacterial sepsis and necrotizing enterocolitis. We recently reported that cord blood monocytes from babies born to obese mothers generate a reduced IL-6/TNFα response to Toll-like receptors 1/2 and 4 ligands compared to those collected from lean mothers. These observations suggest altered intrauterine development of the offspring’s immune system, which in turn results in dysregulated function. We, therefore, investigated transcriptional and epigenetic differences within cord blood monocytes stratified by pre-pregnancy maternal body mass index. We show that cord blood monocytes from babies born to obese mothers generate a dampened response to LPS stimulation compared to those born to lean mothers, both at the level of secreted immune effectors and transcriptional activity. Since gene expression profiles of resting cord blood monocytes from both groups were comparable, we next investigated the role of epigenetic differences. Indeed, we detected stark differences in methylation levels within promoters/regulatory regions of genes involved in Toll-like receptor signaling in resting cord blood monocytes. Interestingly, DNA methylation status of resting cells was more predictive of transcriptional changes post stimulation, suggesting cytosine methylation as one of the dominant mechanisms driving functional inadequacy in cord blood monocytes. These data highlight a potentially critical role of maternal obesity-induced epigenetic changes in influencing the function of offspring’s monocytes at birth. These findings further our understanding of mechanisms that explain the increased risk of infection in neonates born to mothers with high pre-gravid body mass index.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":31,"study_labels":[{"level":"study","event_code":"ACUTE_BACTERIAL_INFECTION","certainty":"suspected","delta_days":-60.0,"delta_bucket":"B_FAR","time_precision":"approx","stage":"teacher","confidence":1.0,"evidence":"Pre-pregnancy maternal obesity is associated with adverse outcomes for the offspring, including increased incidence of neonatal bacterial sepsis and necrotizing enterocolitis.","model_version":"teacher:mlx:mlx-community/Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct-4bit"}],"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN07512879","study_accession":"PRJNA398556","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","description":"R212-L7-P6-CAGATC-Sequences.txt.gz","sex":"male","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2017-11-09","attributes":[["isolate","new born cordblood RNA"],["age","0 years"],["biomaterial_provider","Ilhem Messaoudi"],["sex","male"],["tissue","new born cordblood"],["cell_type","monocyte"],["treatment","Lean"],["Sample Info","B012-Resting-RNASEQ"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR5939980","experiment_accession":"SRX3098547","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":28789364,"base_count":2878936400,"first_public":"2017-11-09","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: Maob monocyte RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN07512880","study_accession":"PRJNA398556","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","description":"R212-L7-P7-ACTTGA-Sequences.txt.gz","sex":"female","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2017-11-09","attributes":[["isolate","new born cordblood RNA"],["age","0 years"],["biomaterial_provider","Ilhem Messaoudi"],["sex","female"],["tissue","new born cordblood"],["cell_type","monocyte"],["treatment","Lean"],["Sample Info","B018-Resting-RNASEQ"